      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is an excellent ethical and analytical point to raise; a \"policy failure\" just because people *feel* hurt doesn't account for the real economic analysis of purchasing power and aggregate welfare. You can definitely strengthen your argument by arguing that the \"success\" or \"failure\" isn't a binary math equation but a political compromise where the efficiency gains were achieved at the disproportionate cost of a specific demographic's well-being. The strongest verdict might be that the TPP was a **net economic success for the broader economy and the very poor (in terms of inflation), but a political and social failure because the domestic working class bore the brunt of the disruptive shocks without receiving their fair share of the benefits.**",
      "source": "target",
